-- title:
--   On a cone pair the kernel dominates the fractional jump kernel up to the constant
-- statement:
--   Let $d \in \mathbb{N}$ and work in $\mathbb{R}^d$. Here $\Gamma$ is a *configuration*: a map assigning to each point $x$ a double cone
--   $\Gamma(x) = V(v,\theta) = \{h \neq 0 : \angle(v,h) < \theta \text{ or } \angle(v,h) > \pi-\theta\}$
--   with unit axis $v$ and apex angle $\theta \in (0,\pi/2]$, and $V^\Gamma[x] = x + \Gamma(x)$ is the cone
--   translated to $x$. The hypothesis `KernelBounds` is assumption (2): $\Lambda \ge 1$, $k$ is symmetric, and
--   $$\Lambda^{-1}\bigl(\mathbf 1_{V^\Gamma[x]}(y)+\mathbf 1_{V^\Gamma[y]}(x)\bigr)\|x-y\|^{-d-\alpha}
--   \;\le\; k(x,y)\;\le\;\Lambda\,\|x-y\|^{-d-\alpha}.$$
--
--   Suppose $x,y \in \mathbb{R}^d$ form a *cone pair*, in the sense that $y \in V^\Gamma[x]$, i.e. $y - x \in \Gamma(x)$. Then
--   $$\|x-y\|^{-d-\alpha} \;\le\; \Lambda\, k(x,y).$$
--   Here $\|x-y\|^{-d-\alpha}$ is the jump kernel of the $H^{\alpha/2}$ seminorm.
--
--   This is the lower bound of assumption (2) read in the direction one actually uses it: the kernel $k$ is degenerate in general — it is only bounded below where the cones see — but on the pairs that a cone does see it is comparable to the isotropic fractional kernel. Every argument that converts a cone-restricted fractional energy into the kernel energy passes through this single pointwise inequality.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The inequality is between elements of `ℝ≥0∞`; the constant enters as `ENNReal.ofReal Λ`, and $\Lambda \ge 1$ is part of the `KernelBounds` structure. Only the cone at $x$ is required to see $y$; the symmetric condition $x \in V^\Gamma[y]$ is not assumed, and the lower bound of (2) — a sum of two indicators — is used through just one of its two terms. Nothing is assumed about $x \neq y$: for $x = y$ the left-hand side is the `rpow` of $0$ at a negative exponent, which in Lean is $0$.
